# Copy into the product repository as .github/CODEOWNERS.
#
# GitHub requests review from the owners of the changed paths when a pull
# request opens, which is what makes review a property of the repository
# rather than something the author has to remember.
#
# Every repository needs its own copy. CODEOWNERS is NOT one of the default
# community health files an organisation's .github repository provides to
# repositories that lack them — that set is CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,
# CONTRIBUTING.md, discussion category forms, FUNDING.yml, issue and pull
# request templates, SECURITY.md and SUPPORT.md. Ownership does not cross
# repository boundaries.
#
# Requesting a review is not requiring one. This file decides WHO is asked;
# the `main` ruleset decides whether the answer is needed before merge. Both
# are needed, and neither substitutes for the other.
#
# ORDER MATTERS, AND IT IS NOT SPECIFICITY. The last matching pattern wins,
# so the catch-all goes FIRST and path-scoped entries go below it. A
# catch-all placed at the bottom would override every specific owner above
# it, which is the opposite of what it looks like it does.
